Visur will have two prompt modes, AI and shell.
It will run as a server which clients attach to and hyperbase is the storage mechanism on the backend.
Eventually, we'll make our own visur terminal client, but that'll be much later at first we'll just continue using built-in terminals.

Visur will be designed to run on MacBook first then we will design the set up for Linux
Visor is the new agent harness. It will merge all the best features of other AI harnesses like pi herdr firstmate pisubagents. Either we will include those orchestras as providers or we will custom build our own providers with the same ideas.

Commands and skills are kind of the same thing in visur. If you want to run an agent skill, it's just a bash command in the shell prompt. When you want to run that skill from inside the AI prompt you just tell AI the command or skill to run.

One of the first commands we have built-in is visur-change. Running this command with some change you want to make to visor will result in bash simpleton scripts being run to Direct AI into looking for the feature you want added to visor or changed and see if there's a way to update the settings to get what you want. If there is no existing setting to get what you want specifically than AI will be required to provide you a list of workarounds substitutions, and then the final option which is to change visur source code and live recompile it to get what you want. So for example, I want my text to be pink in certain scenarios so advisor looks for any settings related to that tells you what it can change and the side effects of that change and maybe none of those features are good enough for me so I tell it no change the source codes, but this feature is supported without the side effects. Then AI will go to work while you do other things to update your visor terminal to get what you want and again it will live rec compile the client for you or if it had to make a change to visur server code then it will live recompile that in a safe way.

So out of the box as soon as people install visor, we want it to have a standard set up that works immediately out of the box without any additional config or set up. As soon as you install visor, you should be able to start developing apps with extreme precision and control over AI with sandboxing parallelism time and token, budgets and supervisors as well as the ability to have fluent conversations with tts and stt, and finally the ability to reach your Pfizer server for any device on your local network via the web browser or SSH, also it will have the ability to send you phone calls, slack, text, email or Google hangout messages depending on what providers you chose during the initial set up, or you could skip that. This works for not just AI prompts but for shell prompts for example maybe you ran a command that takes multiple hours to run. You will get a notification ding as soon as that command completes. Then if you're in AI prompt mode, the AI will prompt you when it's waiting for you to give feedback. Another out of the box feature that needs to work is local ticketing systems, which can be pushed to a repo if the user gives AI that power. we automatically tell users what kind of scope their tokens need to have for operations on github, and this will be well protected via the sandboxing, and we can even make simpleton scripts which do the work instead of AI, so AI might not be allowed to run the 'git push' or any remote commands at all if you want to prevent it, but the simpleton bash commands can. Simpleton / AI will use the git worktree to make local references where AI can work and not mess with the current local branch, then get approval from the supervisor AI to merge. The supervisor AI is configured to give you regular updates via phone messages. At first, we won't re-invent the wheel, we'll just use a modified version of firstmate to do this task. In the future we'll create our own lightweight provider via simpleton. Simpleton can easily do all the work of supervising AI.

Visur AIs will always prioritize local information, data and sources or tools. So any information that gives you is based on your current system and any recommendations are also based on your current system. It will also reach out to external sources after it has confirmed everything locally just as a backup to its conclusions and ass assertions. All AI claims and assertions that it gives the user will come with the source links for the user to use to validate what the AI said is true.

Visor will already come configured with whatever local models it can run based on your local machines power. The user can optionally configure this to use different models with different settings or use cloud models instead, and they will be asked to provide the API key in those instances, but the nice thing about Pfizer is that it will be able to run the inference models locally without you having to think about anything. He will tell you all the default and let you choose. So for example, since my machine is a Mac with 64 gigs of unified memory and 400 MB per second memory bandwidth, it will conclude that it can run Qwen 35B in omlx and it will configure openviking, our modified versions of Matt, lavish, teach, and wiki skills. It will also configure text to speech and speech to text as well as the fluency plug-in whatever that is to make sure that the conversations with visur / AI are fluent. And again any of those local AI services can be replaced with cloud service services in the confit settings

I want to mention something else visor will not have a plug-in system. That's not how Pfizer is designed if you ever want to change something about visor you need to use your skills and utilize the AI agents to change the source code of visor live and refresh it there will not be any plug-in systems. This way each individual copy of visur is custom built for your needs. You don't have to worry about learning some complicated plugin lanuage or settings config locations. You just have to know the code for visur and if you know the code for visur, you'll be able to make visur your own.



