Rough Draft workflow for AI programming

Visur will instruct users how to begin using visur for optimal AI / Human hybrid work. Included are the human habits listed below and the workflow that simpleton + AI work through with the user

human habits:
- Once per week do a 1-3 hour session of ai task making and every day do 15 min
- Always validate AI claims by going to it's provided local and external sources. Make sure it's not getting things wrong
- Once per day spend 1-2 hours studying the lessons provided by AI about your study topics and skills you want to improve
- Once per day spend 30-60 min unblocking tickets for AI on various projects
AI management workday= 1 hour 45 min to 3 hours 15 min

Preparation, generate skeleton ideas: (use knowledge-hub or a visur excel sheet)
- Human: Basically create a list of goals / projects and come up with a bunch of tasks as many as you can think of based on your goals.
- Human: Add all the sources of truth and resources which must be available to the AI for use in accomplishing the tasks in the project.
- Human / AI: If you need to create wiki pages or ingest openviking data as aids for AI to improve accuracy, speed and optimize context usage, then do that now.
- Human: Provide a list of /teach topics you want AI to teach you. It will make those into tickets and start making those lessons along the way for you to open up with an agent in parallel whenever you are ready to study the topic. These lesson documents automatically get updated by the AI whenever  something changes in their source material or new research data comes in. So for example, you tell AI you want to understand how the code in your repo works, but there is no code yet. So you tell AI at what stage you want the lessons created, maybe after beta / v1 of the project is finished. It creates 5 lesson tickets with a generic itinerary, and then when it finishes v1 of the project, it will automatically begin working on those lessons. You will be able to start learning from those lessons in parallel while AI gets going on V2. And you don’t have to tell AI to do anything for V2, it will automatically create those ‘documentation’ tickets and update the lessons based on changes happening in V2.

Body
For anything foundational that absolutely must be right:
- Human / AI: go through grilling and wayfinder sessions until the idea is fully fleshed out. No longer a skeleton. Otherwise just provide the skeleton ideas and have AI generate a bunch of tickets. Use the updated grilling and wayfinder skills which create my new ticket ideas
- AI: Always make these types of tickets: Research, implement / documentation and teach. The research tickets are for things you and the AI don't know enough about yet and need to understand better.
- AI: Every ticket needs a fresh session / context. A single ticket should always be doable in a single session with AI. If not, it needs to be broken into parts. Multiple tickets should be doable in parallel.

Research Tickets:
- AI: Use experimentation processes and where needed create tickets for human to run experiments in parallel. If you've done all you can and just waiting on human experiments, change ticket to "blocker waiting on human" with the linked human experiment ticket that is blocking and start a new context / subagent to work on another project or task.
- Human: work on any experiment tasks given to you by the AI and let it
- AI: Document your experiments and findings in wiki pages or openviking.
- Human: Document your experiments and findings using visur / knowledge hub.
- AI: Once all necessary experiments are documented compile an overview doc outlining everything in detail with links to the original source materials
- Human: review the overview and conclusion doc and determine if more research is necessary or not. If not, then just proceed with the next part. Otherwise tell AI your thoughts and go through more research

Implementation tickets:
- AI: Look over the code or project using whatever source materials provided and only obtain the context necessary. Start sub-agents and give them necessary instructions with context for implementing the ticket in multiple parts if needed. review their work and approve or disprove based on the results or validation checks you use
- AI: Set status to ‘blocked for human review’
- Human: While AI is working on implementation, work the same amount you did before using AI. Only now, your focus is on gaining deeper understanding of the code, so study any docs, /teach lessons, or create more tasks for AI to work on with the current or other projects.
- Human: Set status to ‘in progress’ and review any PRs or changes raised by AI. Approve or reject. Set status to ‘complete’ if you approve, or ‘needs change’ with your notes explaining everything AI needs to do for you to consider this for approval.
- AI: Periodically check all tickets status and any that are in ‘needs change’ should be looked at in order of priority. Review the feedback given by the user and work on meeting the requirements given for approval. Once done, move ticket back to ‘blocked for human review’
- Human: Review changes again and if everything looks good, approve, or else repeat the previous steps.

Documentation Tickets:
- AI: After completing any implementation ticket, your job is to update any relevant documentation or /teach lessons that relies on the source material that has been changed. Whether it’s ./raw files or ./src or even discoveries of broken / updated links / processes external to the current project such as github.com changing their name to gh.com or something like that you may discover during ticket implementation.
- AI: set status to ‘blocked for human review’
- Human: review the changes to docs and approve or reject

Teach Tickets:
- AI: Use lavish-teach skill to generate an html doc with styles for the user to review at their convenience.
- AI: Start a separate sub-agent with its only context being the source materials, and have it review the lavish document to ensure no false information is being taught.
- AI: change status to ‘blocked by human review’.
- Human: Move ticket into ‘in progress’ and begin studying the lesson either with AI present or without, depending on capacity. Practice any core concepts taught and validate yourself by going to source materials and studying valid resources online or from experts as well. You should be spending 4 hours per day studying lessons so you really understand the project, it’s code and how it’s designed / maintained.


How AI actually works in this context:
- Human: starts herdr / pi agent harness in firstmate or some similar orchestration layer
- Human: Runs the new skill which starts the lightweight firstmate / project-manager.
- AI: Based on the list of projects provided by the user and their priority, AI starts each phase (user planning, making tickets, implementation)
- Human: Does the joint planning sessions with AI
- Human: once AI starts implementation phase, always ensure either there are enough tasks for AI to work on in case they complete or get blocked on the others.

